ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS 
Resources allocated by Government for Population Census work is recognition of the 
importance of information collected during the Country’s biggest data collection 
exercise. The key to evidence-based policy formulation is good data and a Population 
Census will remain a key source. Part 1 of the report (Administrative report) serves to 
provide information on the census activities while Part 2 of the report contains tables 
covering population distribution and change, functioning challenges, labour force, 
education, internal migration and Housing.   
The Administrative Report and General Tables was prepared by the Household Survey 
Unit under the leadership and guidance of the head of department, the Government 
Statistician (Census Commissioner). I commend the effort of Maria Musudroka (PS 
HSU) and her core team of Mosese Qaloewai (Data Analyst), Harieta Sefeti (AS IT), 
Anare Motokula (SS Demography), Poasa Naimila (AS GIS), Salanieta Tubuduadua 
(AS Vital) and Mr. William Walker (UNFPA Inforgraphics and Designer consultant). 
The whole department approach towards the national exercise also contributed to staff 
development. It is in this regard that I acknowledge the effort of all the nine 
subcommittes who were tasked with specific activities.  They were the Mapping 
committee, led by Mr. Sole Tubanaika, the Budget committee, led by Ms. Amrita Josu, 
the Human resource committee, led by Ms. Filomena Browne, the Questionnaire 
design committee, led by Ms. Raikoti, the Publicity committee, led by Ms. Emali 
Soderberg, the Training committee, led by Ms. Kelera Nokelevu, the Purchase and 
Equipment (IT) committee, led by Mr. Reenesh Latchman, and the Data processing, 
Data analysis & dissemination committee led by Mr. Jone Fifita. 
I acknowledge the efforts of the senior officers at the Household Survey Unit Hakim 
Mohammed (SS Nadi), Sereima Rokobuli (FS Ba), Avinesh Prasad (SS Northern), Emily 
Dutt (SS Central/Eastern) for their coordination roles at the respective divisional 
offices during the census operation.  A special thank you goes to all the department 
staff’s who played a role in this national exercise.  In particular, the Household survey 
unit staff, Area coordinators, IT support staff, Supervisors and Enumerators for their 
commitment and valuable inputs towards the success of the project.  Most importantly 
is my humble and sincere gratitude to all respective households who participated in 
the interviews. 
The contributions of key development partners, line ministries and individuals are also 
acknowledged.  To ILO, Ms. Marie Fatiaki on Child Labour training, Ms. Surkafa 
Katafono as the National Programme Officer and Mr. Tite Habiyakere with trainining 
on the ICLS concept.  To SPC (Pacific Community), Mr. Toga Raikoti on the 
Questionnaire design, CAPI (WB) training, Mr. Phil Bright on Mapping, Mr. Arthur 
Jorari as a Census technical adviser and Ms. Kim Robertson on Gender Perspective 
training.  To UNFPA, Ms. Sandra Paradez and Ms. Virisila Raitamata on their 
assistance with the dissemination of outputs.  To ABS, Mr. Peter Harper as a census 
technical adviser, Mr. Stephen Wellington on IT environment management and Ms. 
