THE 2017 POPULATION AND HOUSING CENSUS 
A Population and Housing Census provides a snapshot of the characteristics of the 
population (size, structure and distribution). The United Nations recommends that 
population censuses be taken at least every 10 years.  It defines the essential features 
of a population and housing censuses as an individual enumeration, that is prevalence 
within a country, is simultaneous and within a defined period. 
Fi
ji has a longer history of census taking than most developing countries. Its first census 
(a mere headcount of the population) was carried out by the British Administration, as 
early as 1881. After this, until 1921, censuses (headcounts) were conducted at an 
interval of 10 years.   
Fo
rmal census taking only started in 1946. This paper based census, conducted soon 
after the end of World War II, provides more detailed information than the previous ones. 
At the time of the 1946 Census, all information was processed, edited, compiled and 
tabulated by hand. The production of very detailed cross tabulations was not feasible. 
In 2007, cross tabulation was feasible, however data processing took time towards the 
release of the Analytical Report, the 14 provincial profiles/reports together with the 
fertility and mortality monographs.  
The use of Tablets for the 2017 Population and Housing Census was aimed at realising 
the proven gains accruing from a digital census. With the Census Information System 
put in place, progress on data collection, data transfer and information checks was to 
be viewed in real-time. 
Some of the important gains are improved data quality and quicker availability of 
information. With a regional spread of the data being received on a continuous basis, 
some tabulations and their graphical representation were produced on the third day of 
data collection. Previously this was only possible a few months after the data collection 
phase. 
This represents a huge leap in terms of the timely availability of data to be checked, 
meaning any inconsistency is picked up quickly and enumerators can correct these 
while they are still in the field. Such a practise is recommended by Census experts but 
which is quite difficult to implement with paper based census collections. 
Given the interplay of official and non-official statistics in a very dynamic information 
market together with the impact of societal changes on statistical apparatus, the 
department needs to continue to assume its role by adapting itself to technological 
innovations for the provision of various and extensive data requests from all 
stakeholders. Bearing in mind, the adequacy, relevance, and reliability, in short - the 
quality of official statistics. 
