BASIC PRINCIPLES OF A CENSUS 
 
All censuses in Fiji so far, have been carried out on a de-facto basis with de-jure 
elements.  In a de-facto census, all persons present in the country at a certain point in 
time (census night) are included in the enumeration. All persons were to be enumerated 
in the household where they happen to be on census night.  
 
All Census personnel were made familiar with the essential characteristics of a Census. 
This was through meetings and regular briefings.  The Seven Basic Principles of Census 
Taking to which its quality should be assessed are; 
 
i. Sponsorship, responsibility, cooperation and objectives 
ii. Universality 
iii. Coverage 
iv. Simultaneity 
v. Individual listing 
vi. Compilation and publication of results for geographic subdivisions and by basic 
variables 
vii. Defined periodicity 
 
LEGAL BASIS AND CONFIDENTIALITY 
 
Census Act 
 
The 2017 Census was conducted under the provisions of the Census Act (appendix 1) 
of 20th July 1946, Ed. 1978 (CAP 71 and 72) which states that; 
 
 “It shall be lawful for the Minister, whenever he may deem it expedient, 
 by order, to direct that a census be taken of the inhabitants of Fiji, and to 
prescribe the time in relation to which the particulars required by the  
census shall be stated, and the date upon which the census is to be taken  
and for that purpose the Public Service Commission shall appoint a Census 
Commissioner.” 
 
The Minister referred to is the Minister for Economy.  
 
It was considered critical that all census staff were aware of the above legal provision 
that authorizes the conduct of a Population and Housing Census. The legal provision 
serves two main purposes, that is to;  
 
- make it obligatory for respondents to provide the required information. 
- balance that obligation with the individual’s right to privacy. 
 
All Census Field Officers were made aware of the Census Act in addition to the 2017 
Census Regulations (appendix 2). 
 
  
2017 Population and Housing Census, Administrative Report, 2018 
Page 6 
 
Confidentiality 
 
In accordance with paragraph 6 of the Census Act, everybody within the scope of the 
Census is legally obliged to provide information that was required. It was important that 
it was clearly understood by all Census staff as well as respondents that the Census 
was not concerned with information about individuals as such but an attempt to collect 
information about the community as a whole and about groups within the community. 
In that connection enumerators were trained to ask questions that are necessary for the 
completion of the enquiry. 
 
All Field Officers engaged during the Census were required to advise respondents that 
all information gathered would be kept confidential.  
 
 
Work plan and Census Calendar 
 
A Census Calendar ( appendix 3) and a Census Processes and Programme Standard 
(SOP) (appendix 4) was used as a guide for all census activities (appendix 5). Monthly, 
weekly and daily work plans complemented the Census calendar and SOP.  Team 
leaders also took heed of the submission of progress reports ( appendix 6) regarding 
activity start dates and targets dates towards submissions to the Ministry of Economy.  
In addition, a Gantt Chart (appendix 7) was also used to monitor the work plan progress 
as per the number of days left to complete a certain task.   
The ALL team was responsible for the monitoring and evaluation of all operational 
activities for the nine subcommittees.  The attachment in the appendix was used by all 
Team leaders to report on progress on a monthly basis.  For every scheduled Census 
committee meeting, all team leaders presented on updates and progress of work for their 
respective teams. 
Having a good understanding of the need to monitor and evaluate respective activities 
was continually emphasized to the Census Committee members.  Risks and challenges 
during the course of the Census Operation were identified beforehand with solutions 
premeditated.   Monitoring by the divisional coordinators and respective team leaders 
gathered useful information that was used to check on the progress of various Census 
activities agains their set objectives.  
It was also the role of the team leaders to draw on the information generated from the 
monitoring process to detect and trending patten that may need immediate intervention 
given their likely or possible impact on the Census exercise.   Towards the end of the 
project, close monitoring of data quality continued, through the data checks and 
verification, to ensure minimal departure from the project’s expectations. The evaluation 
format via information generated by the Survey Solutions Software were simple – giving 
the date, time, place and the names of the people involved in a certain activity which 
was really useful when a decision needed to be made. 
The very nature of the Census project and programme management required that 
certain meetings be convened when needed in order to communicate and share project 
