OVERALL PLANNING 
 
Preparation for the 2017 Census started in March 2016. Nine sub-committees covering 
key Census Activities were formed and membership comprised personnel from the 
various divisions within FBoS. The sub-committee members were required to continue 
with their normal work. The nine sub-committees were as follows; 
 
1) Budget 
2) Human Resources 
3) Mapping 
4) Training 
5) Publicity 
6) Questionnaire Design 
7) Purchase and Equipment 
8) Data Processing and Tabulation 
9) Data Analysis 
 
The sub-committees were interdependent which required strong coordination and 
interaction amongst members. 
The Fiji Census model approach required a whole department effort. Each phase of the 
Census is dependent on a preceding phase, with the quality of the output from each 
phase having a direct effect on the success of the next phase. 
Resources were identified and organised such that an Activity and Logistics Level (ALL) 
team was established to coordinate the work to be undertaken. The team was also 
responsible for an integrated Census timetable and the Budget submissions for two 
financial years were aligned to the activity schedule formulated by the team. There was 
a high degree of enthusiasm and commitment for the Census throughout the 
Department. 
The Census Order was issued by the Minister for Economy in July 2016. The 
proclamation provided the legal basis for conducting the 2017 Population and Housing 
Census between 16 September 2017 and 16 October 2017.  The Government 
Statistician (GS), Mr. Epeli Waqavonovono, was then appointed as the Census 
Commissioner by the Public Service Commission (PSC). The Census Commissioner then 
issued the Census Regulations. 
Advice and feedback from consultations with relevant experts were taken into account 
before the Government Statistician recommended the use of Tablets in the 2017 
Population Census. This was approved by the Minister for Economy and arrangements 
followed for the purchase of 3,100 tablets for use in data collection.  Study Missions to 
Vanuatu and Tonga confirmed Fiji’s readiness to use tablets which improves the 
reliability and timeliness of Census data. 
The Advantages of a Digital Census: 
 
1) Can provide immediate checks on routing, ranges, and consistency during the 
interview. 
2) Can provide faster and more consistent field editing. 
2017 Population and Housing Census, Administrative Report, 2018 
Page 11 
 
3) Can provide faster and more consistent editing of aggregated data. 
4) Should provide higher data quality. 
5) Faster availability of final data. 
6) No paper questionnaires to manage. 
7) Data is stored, encrypted, safe and secure. 
8) Data is backed up in areas without Network Coverage.  
9) Easy to download data whenever required. 
 
Software Used 
 
The World Bank’s Survey Solution Software was used. The free Software has been 
developed over the years and is being continuously updated. Key enabling features have 
been developed to; 
 
a. Allow better communication between the enumerators and supervisors for checks 
and monitoring purposes. 
b. Produce more reliable statistics due to checks performed during the interview. 
c. Have statistics available in a timely manner due to a reduced time lag between data 
collection and data analysis. 
d. Eliminate the need to scan, verify, code and edit data.  
 
Further details are provided in Section VIII of the Report. 
 
An integrated Census timetable was put in place to effectively monitor and coordinate 
the work overseen by the sub-committees. Key milestones and critical dependencies 
were identified, understood and actively managed to ensure there was no slip-up. Due 
to the size and complexity of the census, some important and complex processes had to 
be refined which in some cases required the mobilization of additional resources. 
The responsibilities of the various sub-committees were demarcated with clearly stated 
objectives.  One of the responsibilities included identifying key stakeholders and 
ensuring that their expertise could be usefully utilized for Census work. 
Effective risk management was considered crucial and quality controls were developed 
and put in place to ensure that the Census data was of the quality expected.  These 
included the management of quality from an end- to-end perspective to ensure a 
successful national data collection exercise.  Comprehensive testing plans were put in 
place with expected outcomes and these were trialled during the Pilot test. 
Key aspects for the whole operation required particular focus in; 
 
- Securing funds 
- Mapping of Enumeration Area Boundaries 
- Procuring, testing and configuring the tablets and associated technologies 
- Designing of the Census Questionnaire via the SPC model 
- Recruiting, training 150 Area Coordinators, 650 Supervisors and 2156 
Enumerators 
- Developing the field procedures, including logistics 
- Developing strategies for non-private dwellings and difficult to enumerate 
population groups 
