Figure 8: Enumeration Area code 
 
 
P P T T S E E E C 
 
− Digits 1 and 2 (PP) identify the province 
− Digits 3 and 4 (TT) identify the tikina vou or combined tikina 
− Digit five (S) identifies the geographic sector. It also identifies the urban area in 
cases where there is more than one urban area in a particular tikina 
− Digits 6, 7 and 8 identify the EA 
− Digit 9 (C) identifies the category or type of EA.  
 
The Mapping Team used tablets for their plotting exercise. A complete household listing 
and headcount was carried out and additional information such as network coverage 
was gathered. Such information was useful in the preparation of small area level 
budgets. 
SPC conducted training in Quantum Geographical information System (QGIS). The 
notion to have this generally used at FBoS was welcomed as it was cost effective.  
Further re-training is planned at FBoS due to the benefits of using QGIS. 
The tikinas were subdivided into (statistical/census) enumeration areas (EA). The EA is 
not an administrative area but purely a statistical/census unit. It is the smallest 
geographic unit (building block) for which census information is published. 
Of the twenty one urban areas, thirteen (13) consisted of an incorporated (gazetted) 
city/town and a peri-urban area. The two (2) cities were Suva and Lautoka and the 
eleven (11) towns were Lami, Nasinu, Nausori, Nadi, Ba, Tavua, Rakiraki Sigatoka, 
Labasa, Savusavu and Levuka. These incorporated cities/towns are subdivided into 
wards.  There were eight (8) urban areas, which did not have an incorporated city/town. 
The urban areas were for census/statistical purposes only. The category included the 
unincorporated townships of Vatukoula, Matei, Korovou, Navua, Pacific Harbour, 
Nabouwalu, Naqara and Seaqaqa. 
The delineation of Urban Boundaries uses the following set of criteria; 
 
1) Urban Attributes 
2) Economic Activity 
3) Population Size 
4) Association and Contiguity with a Town or City 
5) Population Density 
 

