Challenges  
 
The last revision of EA boundaries was in 2004-2005.  With changes continually 
occurring, a lot of boundaries had to be revised so the mapping team had to identify 
suitable physical and natural features that the EA boundaries had to follow. So some of 
the challenges encountered during the exercise were as follows; 
1. Isolated roads in the rural areas which had not been maintained for a long period 
of time. 
2. Uncertainty of road conditions.  In most instances, vehicles got stuck on mud 
tracks. 
3. Lack of communication with HQ and other teams due to poor phone reception 
in remote rural areas, after teams were sent out  
4. Poor network communication with maritime villages  
5. Accessibility to villages in the interiors of Fiji through river crossings 
 
  

2017 Population and Housing Census, Administrative Report, 2018 
Page 31 
 
Figure 13: Road access a challenge 
    
6. Time Constraints – The revision of urban boundaries is challenging. For instance, 
the extension of the Urban areas of Nadi, Lautoka and Sigatoka were major exercises 
given that the numerous Enumeration Areas were affected hence their boundaries 
had to be re- drawn. Numerous visits were needed before a clear boundary 
demarcation could be finalized. 
 
The need to revise Urban Boundaries arises when; 
• Legal town or city boundaries are extended 
• Rural areas are developed to urban status i.e. meet the criteria to be 
classified as urban. 
 

