− All trainees to be thoroughly familiar with the contents of the enumerator and 
supervisor manual. 

2017 Population and Housing Census, Administrative Report, 2018 
Page 50 
 
− All trainees sat the required examination on August 18th and that their selection 
to be based on their overall performance during the training and examination 
results. 
 
− During the three weeks period after Census Night (18 September to 6 October 
2017), all Enumerators to have collected and recorded all the required 
information from households in their respective EAs.  
 
− Enumerators to continuously sync completed questionnaires from the field to the 
Supervisors who upon approval will send it to the AC at HQ level.  
 
− For any unanswered questions or errors on the filled forms, the AC was to reject 
the form and return it to the Supervisor who sends it to the enumerator in the 
field to amend and resend. 
 
Reserved enumerators were used; 
− To assist in the Enumeration of the largest and/or most populous EAs. 
 
− When a regular enumerator needed to be replaced because of illness, non-
performance or inappropriate behaviour.  
 
− For enumeration in areas where work was progressing more slowly than 
expected, for instance, due to adverse weather conditions or other challenging 
situations. 
 
− To help out in the enumeration of Institutionalized persons in non-private 
dwellings (NPDs) i.e. Hospitals and Other Health Facilities, Correction 
institutions, Boarding Schools, Single Officers Barracks, Work Camps etc. as 
well as the enumeration of hotel/motel guests.  
 
All equipment including tablets, charger, external battery, map(s), identification card 
etc. had to be returned to the supervisor after the operation. 
 
NPDs/Other Institutions 
 
It was the role of the lead trainer to ensure the proper count of Non private dwellings 
(NPD) and or other institutions. The key responsibilities of the institutional coordinators 
were clearly articulated as per the following; 
− To complete the questionnaire as soon as possible after census night. 
 
− To ensure that the name of the institution (or ship) were written at the top of the 
questionnaire to distinguish it from a private household. 
 
2017 Population and Housing Census, Administrative Report, 2018 
Page 51 
 
− To communicate with the responsible AC and supervisor on certain areas that 
needed clarity. 
 
− To ensure that the submitted questionnaire was checked for consistency and 
accuracy. 
 
− To note any issues encountered and have clear comments with instructions for the 
Supervisor whilst collecting the questionnaire. 
 
KEY PERFORMANCE INDICATORS 
 
The following had to be considered before the payment of institutional coordinators; 
− Questionnaire filled within the scheduled time. 
− Quality of data collected. 
− All institutional dwellers were enumerated. 
 
Training by Key Stakeholders and Consultants 
 
Throughout the preparatory phase, key stakeholders and consultants were formally 
requested to present to the Area Coordinators on the questionnaire modules. This was 
done to provide an in-depth understanding of the series of questions to obtain the 
required information from households.  They were then expected to deliberate on this 
whilst conducting their own training at the various training centres.   
 
1. Ministry of Employment Productivity and Industrial Relations (MEPIR) 
 
For any field operation, the safety of Field staff is paramount.  Through the 
MPEIR awareness sessions, the AC’s were informed of the Health and Safety 
at Work Act of 1996 and more.   
 
 
The awareness included the following; 
1. Training Regulations 1997 
2. Administration Regulations 1997 
3. Fees Schedule 
4. General Workplace Conditions Regulations 2003 
5. Control of Hazardous Substances Regulations 2006 
6. Diving Regulations 2006 
7. HIV/AIDS at Workplace 2008 
8. Sexual Harassment at Workplace (COP) 
 
 
The AC’s gained from the MEPIR OHS awareness sessions through their; 
- clear understanding of the importance of health & safety in the workplace 
- their ability to identify the different types of hazards in the workplace 

2017 Population and Housing Census, Administrative Report, 2018 
